1
00:00:01,710 --> 00:00:04,410
So now we will see the concept of value blocks.

2
00:00:04,410 --> 00:00:11,550
So value blocks are also data blocks, but they are special types of data blocks which you have to create

3
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:12,400
on your own.

4
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,360
We will see how we can create value blocks as well.

5
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:23,430
So value blocks are the blocks whenever you need integers in a form of increment or decrement.

6
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:31,890
Let's suppose you want to create an application where you want to store integers which will be increased

7
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:39,990
on certain condition and will be decreased on certain condition, like storing points or storing credit

8
00:00:39,990 --> 00:00:40,600
values.

9
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:45,280
So these are all integers which can be increment or decrement as well.

10
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:45,640
Right.

11
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:52,080
So it depends on the condition it will be on the increment side and it can also be on the decrement

12
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:52,630
side, right?

13
00:00:52,770 --> 00:00:57,810
So it depends on the condition you can use these value blocks.

14
00:00:58,020 --> 00:01:02,560
So value blocks are created on any data block.

15
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:07,960
We can create value block on any data block and value block is of four bytes.

16
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:13,210
As you can see over here, the value block is of four bytes.

17
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:18,300
Then the same value block is repeated in the complement format.

18
00:01:19,050 --> 00:01:19,230
Right.

19
00:01:19,290 --> 00:01:26,940
So after four bytes, the starting from fourth till seventh byte, it's the complement of that bytes

20
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,520
of that integer, I mean to say.

21
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:35,990
So the value is stored in the first four bytes and then the complement of that integer.

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,460
Then again, this integer is repeated.

23
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:40,840
This is how value blocks work.

24
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:41,190
Right.

25
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:44,130
So this is how the structure of value blocks is created.

26
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:50,250
And the last four blocks are used to store the address of these three blocks.

27
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:51,920
Right.

28
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,710
So this is how value blocks work.

29
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:58,390
First, we create an integer of four bytes.

30
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:01,040
Then there is a complement of that integer.

31
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:05,030
And after the complement, the value is again repeated.

32
00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:12,080
And then the address of the of these three blocks are in the last four blocks.

33
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:14,740
So this is how value blocks work.

34
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:18,830
We will see the application of value blocks practically in our coming videos.

